# Both `terminate <job id>` and `$host.signal <pid>` must be able to kill a running
# *external* job -- the former through crush's own job-control plumbing, the latter by
# sending a real OS signal directly to the process, bypassing that plumbing entirely.
#
# Checking the killed job's status must filter down to that specific job's own row via
# `jobs | where {($id == ...)} | select status`, not just grep the rendered screen for
# the word "Terminated" -- same reasoning as `pty_bg_resumes_without_foregrounding.crush`.
# It must go further here, though: this script performs *two* such checks (one per
# mechanism), so by the time the second one runs, the word "Terminated" has already
# legitimately appeared once on the growing screen from the first check -- `wait_for`'s
# plain-string match is "does this text appear *anywhere* on the screen", so a second
# bare `wait_for "Terminated"` would trivially re-match that earlier occurrence and pass
# even if the second mechanism (`$host.signal`) never worked at all. Confirmed directly:
# temporarily disabling just the `$host.signal`-path fix (`spawn_child_wait`'s `Signaled`
# arm in `src/builtins/control/cmd.rs`) while leaving `terminate`'s own fix intact still
# made this test pass, for exactly that reason, when the second check used a bare
# `wait_for "Terminated"`.
#
# Each check's own status is instead formatted into a distinct, single-use marker string
# (`TERM_CHECK_<status>` / `SIG_CHECK_<status>`) built from the *actual* captured status
# value, and `wait_for` is given that whole marker -- so a passing match can only mean
# that specific check's own `jobs | where {($id == ...)}` row genuinely read back
# "Terminated" at the point it ran, not a stale echo of the other check's marker (the two
# prefixes are textually disjoint) or of the word "Terminated" by itself.
#
# Job id `0`, both times: confirmed directly against the debug binary (see
# `pty_bg_resumes_without_foregrounding.crush` for why ids are deterministic for a given,
# fixed script structure). The first `cmd sleep 100 &` is the first job ever started in
# this fresh nested session, so it gets id 0; the `terminate 0`/status-check commands
# that follow are themselves short-lived jobs that finish and get reaped well within the
# counted prompts below -- so the *second* `cmd sleep 101 &` also lands back on id 0.
#
# `$host.procs`'s `name` column is the process's own *executable path* (e.g. `/bin/sleep`,
# not the bare string `"sleep"` the plan's original brief guessed), and on a machine
# running other tests concurrently there can be more than one `/bin/sleep` process alive
# at once (including a stray already-dead one sysinfo can still briefly report) -- so the
# filter also requires `$ppid == ($t.pid())` (this nested session's own pid, queryable
# directly from the outer script -- no round trip through the nested session's own
# screen needed) to uniquely identify *this* script's own backgrounded `sleep`, not some
# unrelated one. Confirmed directly: without the `ppid` half of the filter, the lookup
# intermittently returned a stale, already-dead pid instead of the real one. Even with
# that filter, the earlier, now-`Terminated` `sleep 100` from the first half is this same
# nested session's own child too, and briefly matches alongside the real `sleep 101` if
# sysinfo's scan catches it mid-reap -- so the result is also sorted by `pid` descending
# before taking the first row, since the later-spawned `sleep 101` always has the higher
# pid. This duplicate-tolerant shape is why this specific lookup isn't routed through the
# shared `pty_child_pid` helper (which asserts exactly one match, fine everywhere else
# this plan uses it, but wrong here) -- it's inlined below instead, with the same
# bounded-retry rationale as `pty_child_pid` itself: the real external process a
# backgrounded `cmd ... &` starts isn't necessarily visible in a fresh `$host.procs` scan
# at the exact instant this runs.
#
# `$host.signal` takes the pid as a plain unnamed argument, not via a piped value -- `pid`
# is an unnamed argument slot the `#[signature]` parser fills positionally, but `host:
# signal`'s own handler never reads from `context.input`, so piping a pid into it (as the
# plan's original brief guessed) is silently accepted but never actually signals
# anything. Confirmed directly: with the pid piped in instead of passed positionally, the
# `sleep` process was still alive afterward (checked via a real `ps`), and the status
# check below never read back `Terminated` for it.
#
# Uses `pty_run`'s counted prompt (see pty_test_helpers.crush) instead of a bare
# `wait_for "# "` plus a settle delay after every write -- a bare `wait_for "# "` can be
# satisfied by a prompt already on screen *before* the write, proving nothing about
# whether the command just written has actually finished; an earlier version of this
# test needed a fixed delay after *every single* write to avoid lines racing the
# nested session's own line-by-line processing (confirmed directly: without one after
# every write, lines typed back-to-back intermittently ended up visibly concatenated/
# corrupted on the rendered screen). A counted prompt removes the need for that
# entirely: `pty_run` returns only once the right prompt has genuinely been rendered,
# so the next write is always safe to send immediately.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "cmd sleep 100 &")
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "terminate 0")
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "$status0 := ($(jobs | where {($id == 0)} | select status))[0].status")
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "echo $(\"TERM_CHECK_{}\".format $status0)")
$t.wait_for "TERM_CHECK_Terminated"

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "cmd sleep 101 &")
$pids := $($list.of 0)
$pid_count := 0
$tries := 0
while {(($tries < 20) and ($pid_count == 0))} {
    $pids = $($host.procs | where {(($ppid == ($t.pid())) and ($name =~ ^(.*sleep)))} | select pid | sort pid reverse=$true | $list.collect pid)
    $pid_count = $($pids | count)
    if ($pid_count == 0) {
        sleep 50ms
        $tries = ($tries + 1)
    }
}
assert ($pid_count > 0) "expected at least one sleep process within the retry budget"
# The highest pid is always the just-started `sleep 101`, never the earlier, by-now-
# Terminated `sleep 100` -- see the header comment above for why both can transiently
# appear together.
$pid := $pids[0]
$host.signal $pid signal=SIGKILL
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "$status1 := ($(jobs | where {($id == 0)} | select status))[0].status")
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "echo $(\"SIG_CHECK_{}\".format $status1)")
$t.wait_for "SIG_CHECK_Terminated"

$t.close
